<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">French Guinea</h1><p class="st-meta">1882 CE – 1958 CE · capital Conakry</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The French colony on the Guinea coast and Futa Jallon highlands, which in 1958 became the only territory to reject de Gaulle's constitution and take immediate independence.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> built posts on the rivers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> coast from the 1860s, and in 1882 grouped them as the Rivieres du Sud, which soon gained an administration separate from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-senegal">French Senegal</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noel-ballay">Noel Ballay</a> the colony was renamed French Guinea in the early 1890s, with its new capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conakry">Conakry</a>. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/imamate-of-futa-jallon">Imamate of Futa Jallon</a> was absorbed in 1896 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samori-ture">Samori Toure</a>'s lands by 1898.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> was part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/french-west-africa">French West Africa</a>. Its economy moved from wild <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a>, collected under heavy taxation, to bananas from plantations and, from the 1950s, bauxite mining. Chiefs appointed by the French collected taxes and recruited forced labour. Most people were Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulbe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandinka">Maninka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susu-people">Susu</a>, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/imamate-of-futa-jallon">Futa Jallon</a> remained a centre of Islamic learning.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmed-sekou-toure">Ahmed Sekou Toure</a> and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-uganda">Democratic Party</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> campaigned against the 1958 constitution, and on 28 September 1958 Guinea voted no. It became independent on 2 October 1958, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> withdrew its staff and aid abruptly.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/french-guinea.jpg" alt="French Guinea" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown Vector: SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_France.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1882 CE – 1958 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Conakry</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Noel Ballay, Ahmed Sekou Toure</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, Pular, Maninka, Susu</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/guinea">Guinea</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Guinee francaise, Rivieres du Sud, Colony of Guinea</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1920&amp;focus=french-guinea" aria-label="French Guinea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4302 1823 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where French Guinea lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4622 2079L4674 2037M4622 2079L5054 728M4622 2079L4523 1888"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4674 2037h0M5054 728h0M4523 1888h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4622 2079h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4622 2079h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Conakry. <a href="/atlas?year=1920&amp;focus=french-guinea">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
